<!-- End 1-001: Divider --> </td></tr><tr><td bgcolor="#FFFFFF"><!-- Start 1-006: Headline | H3 --><section id="1006_h3" layout-row="true"><div layout-row="true"><table layout-row="true" role="presentation" width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> <tbody><tr layout-row="true"> <td valign="top" align="left"> <table role="presentation" width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" class="wrap"> <tbody><tr> <td align="left" style="padding: 0px 20px 16px 20px" class="pad20rl match"> <h3 style="font-size: 14px; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; line-height: 120%; margin: 0px; font-weight: normal; letter-spacing: 0px; text-transform: none; color: #000000; font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" enable-personalization="true" data-richtext="h3_text" data-groupid="h3" class="mobile_match"><strong><span style="font-size: 18px; line-height: 120%;"><span style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 120%;"><strong><span style="font-family: Georgia, Times, serif; line-height: 120%;"><a style="color: #000000; font-weight: inherit; background: none; text-decoration: none;" href="https://email.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/why-its-even-more-important-to-consider-restructuring-before-spin-offs?__hScId__=v710000019e67efa8bfbc29776e96c65848&__hRlId__=e8bc20255de04fe70000021ef3a0bce4&__hDId__=e8bc2025-5de0-4fe7-9268-aeba118617bd&__hSD__=d3d3Lm1ja2luc2V5LmNvbQ==&cid=mgp_opr-eml-nsl-omcknsl-mgp-glb--&hlkid=7db054da46a44560b18ddcb691a60787&hctky=17208988&hdpid=e8bc2025-5de0-4fe7-9268-aeba118617bd" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-link-id="e79b5da0d15f11ec8a1b4b78ed446cb7">Breaking up to grow﻿</a></span></strong></span><br></span></strong></h3> </td> </tr> </tbody></table> </td> </tr> </tbody></table> </div> </section> <!-- End 1-006: Headline | H3 --> </td></tr><tr><td bgcolor="#FFFFFF"><!-- Start 1-008: Paragraph | P1 --><section id="1008_p1" layout-row="true"><div layout-row="true"><table layout-row="true" role="presentation" width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> <tbody><tr layout-row="true"> <td valign="top" align="left"> <table role="presentation" width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" class="wrap"> <tbody><tr> <td align="left" style="padding: 0px 20px 16px 20px" class="pad20rl match"><p style="font-size: 14px; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; line-height: 150%; margin: 0px; font-weight: normal; letter-spacing: 0px; text-transform: none; color: #333333; font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" enable-personalization="true" data-richtext="p1_text" data-groupid="p1" class="mobile_match"><span style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 150%;"><strong>In the news.</strong> Corporate spin-offs are gaining momentum in the market, Bloomberg reports. In the first quarter of 2026, one benchmark showed that US spin-off stocks outperformed the S&amp;P 500 by their widest margin in six years. This trend reflects a shift away from the long-held view that scale and diversification necessarily produce greater shareholder value. Now, investors seem to prefer simpler, more-focused businesses with clearer strategies and operations, especially if the spin-off is a well-known asset in its own right. But breakups also carry risks, including debt burdens, greater exposure to volatility, and potential exclusion from major indexes that attract institutional investors. [<a href="https://email.mckinsey.com/T/v710000019e67efa8bfbc29776e96c65848/e8bc20255de04fe70000021ef3a0bce5/e8bc2025-5de0-4fe7-9268-aeba118617bd?__dU__=v0G4RBKTXg2GsTlMa8YbE0npiPHb7Gzq29ujr-mrJsl6bcn0E30HNSquNCCrd1XNAz&__F__=v0fUYvjHMDjRPMSh3tviDHXIoXcPxvDgUUCCPvXMWoX_0JoZLAZABQF8G5gOH5oFN1N6-YCrc_3ImoVgYYBTu8FLbZPH2jWToOBEs_ts-n8J80KbWwU4Vr9uHsHqc9fn7_5PonjzZ-zcT34lvDHyJ4cdzHSYVMqdjUxjQn6sXOltBaRErMDTA4u8jioWhbRxonGHIr_9d57qdApC_YHj4gIYLj2mUV01jo3ctGTSTknTZOwBj3J-3fRdQHGyO2N79Hm_19x_vCYiFJUe6SXKHtS8CZMzC2yHhtwu2E8R08afaMXqpWUVWJWUQGhcZQbGUwCPgYrNb8FBZQcWW7lqSBIX58A0m6ag6u7DzRmhLon